; What the symbols of @CALCULUS@.eo mean, as a transformation into the deep
; embedding of SMT-LIB that the correctness proof is stated against.
;
; Both symbols transform pointwise into the SMT-LIB symbol of the same name,
; which is what an arity written on its own says. That is the whole file: a
; calculus this small has nothing else to say about what it means.
;
; The SMT-LIB semantics this is written against is smt.eos, which is the same
; for every calculus over ordinary SMT-LIB and is not this file's to change.

(define-symbol not (x))

(define-symbol and (x y))

(define-symbol => (x y))

; Required whatever the calculus declares.
;
; The pipeline asks the semantics which datatype names are reserved, even for a
; calculus with no datatypes -- so a signature that has none still has to answer.
; `:keep` is what says the block stands regardless of what the input declares,
; since what names this program is the pipeline rather than a symbol of the
; signature.
;
; This is item 1 of docs/eoc-requests.md seen from the other side: the datatype
; machinery is not optional, so the *semantics* is not allowed to omit it either.
; Copied from the CPC semantics, where the same block appears for the same
; reason.
(program $eo_to_smt_reserved_datatype_name ((s "String"))
  :keep
  :signature ("String") "Bool"
  (
  (($eo_to_smt_reserved_datatype_name s) ("string_prefix_eq" "str_at_sign" s))
  )
)
